Due to separate excitation, the flux is constant. By KVL at rated current, \[E = V_{t}-I_{a}R_{a}
=200-(10\times 2)
=180\,\text{V}\] Since, \[E=k\phi\omega\] At rated speed of \(2000\,\text{rpm}\), \[k\phi=\frac{E}{\omega}
=\frac{180}{\dfrac{2\pi}{60}\times 2000}
=0.8598\,\text{V}\cdot\text{s/rad}\] Developed torque, \[T=k\phi I_{a}\] When the motor draws \(5\,\text{A}\) from the source, \[T=0.8598\times 5
=4.299
\approx 4.3\,\text{N}\cdot\text{m}\] \[\boxed{A}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Developed torque is proportional to armature current: \[T=k\phi I_{a}\]</li><li>Torque varies linearly with armature current when flux is constant.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use terminal voltage directly in the torque equation; first calculate back emf.</li><li>Convert speed from rpm to rad/s before evaluating \(k\phi\).</li><li>Do not neglect armature resistance drop while calculating \(E\).</li><li>Use the new armature current (\(5\,\text{A}\)) while computing torque.</li></ul></div>

