The torque developed in an electrical machine is proportional to the product of air-gap flux and rotor (or armature) current. \[T \propto \phi I_{2}'\] Since the motor draws rated current in both cases, \[I_{2}'=\text{constant}\] Also, \[\phi \propto \frac{V}{f}\] For a constant supply voltage of \(400\,\text{V}\), \[\phi \propto \frac{1}{f}\] Therefore, \[T \propto \phi
\propto \frac{1}{f}\] Comparing the two operating frequencies, \[\frac{T_{75}}{T_{50}}
=
\frac{\phi_{75}}{\phi_{50}}
=
\frac{50}{75}
=
\frac{2}{3}\] Hence, the torque developed at \(75\,\text{Hz}\), \(400\,\text{V}\) is less than the torque developed at \(50\,\text{Hz}\), \(400\,\text{V}\). \[\boxed{A}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Electromagnetic torque is proportional to the product of air-gap flux and rotor current: \[T \propto \phi I_{2}'\]</li><li>Air-gap flux is approximately proportional to the \(V/f\) ratio: \[\phi \propto \frac{V}{f}\]</li><li>For constant voltage operation, increasing frequency reduces flux and hence reduces torque capability.</li></ul></div>

