j0.12 &amp; j0.16 &amp; j0.34
\end{bmatrix}\] The pre-fault voltages are 1.0 pu at all the buses. The system was unloaded prior to the fault. A solid three-phase fault takes place at bus 2.<br/><br/>A power system consists of 2 areas (Area 1 and Area 2) connected by a single tie line. It is required to carry out a load flow study on this system. While entering the network data, the tie-line data (connectivity and parameters) is inadvertently left out. If the load flow program is run with this incomplete data

questions.explanation:
The original system consists of two interconnected areas joined through a tie-line. Due to omission of the tie-line data, the network is interpreted by the load-flow program as two electrically isolated subnetworks. Consequently, \[\text{Area 1}
\quad \text{and} \quad
\text{Area 2}\] become two independent systems. For a load-flow problem to possess a valid reference for voltage angle and real-power mismatch balancing, each electrically isolated network must contain one slack bus. If only one slack bus is specified for the entire system, then one of the isolated areas will not have any slack bus. Therefore, the power-flow equations for that area become ill-posed and the Jacobian matrix becomes singular. Hence, the load-flow solution cannot converge. Therefore, when the tie-line data is omitted and only one slack bus is specified, Load-flow will not converge and the correct answer is \[\boxed{\text{(D The load-flow will not converge if only one slack bus is specified.)}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Removing the tie-line separates the system into two disconnected islands.</li><li>Every electrically isolated island requires its own slack bus.</li><li>A slack bus provides the reference angle and balances real/reactive power mismatches.</li><li>Load-flow convergence requires each island to be properly referenced.</li></ul></div>

