For a three-phase fully controlled thyristor bridge rectifier operating with continuous current and neglecting overlap, \[V_{dc}
=
V_{d0}\cos\alpha.\] The displacement angle of the AC-side current is \[\phi=\alpha.\] The apparent power drawn from the AC supply is \[S
=
\sqrt3V_LI_L.\] For continuous current operation, \[I_L
=
\sqrt{\frac23}\,I_d.\] Since the AC supply voltage and the DC current are constant, \[S=\text{constant}.\] The active and reactive powers are therefore \[P
=
S\cos\alpha,\] and \[Q
=
S\sin\alpha.\] Since \(S\) is constant, \[Q\propto\sin\alpha.\] Now, \[\sin5^\circ
&lt;
\sin10^\circ
&lt;
\sin15^\circ
&lt;
\sin30^\circ.\] Therefore, \[Q_{\max}
\text{ occurs at }
\alpha=30^\circ.\] Hence, the reactive power absorbed from the AC system is maximum when \[\boxed{\alpha=30^\circ}\] Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\text{(B) Reactive power absorbed by the rectifier is maximum when }\alpha=30^\circ.}\]

In a three-step distance protection scheme: <ul><li>Zone-1 acts as the primary protection and operates without intentional time delay.</li><li>Zone-2 acts as backup protection for the adjacent line section.</li><li>Zone-3 provides remote backup protection.</li></ul> For the given system, both relays \(R_1\) and \(R_2\) operate in Zone-2 as backup protection for faults lying in the overlapping backup region. To ensure proper relay co-ordination, backup relays protecting the same region must have identical Zone-2 operating times. Therefore, \[TZ2_{R1}=TZ2_{R2}\] Among the given options, only \[TZ2_{R1}=0.3\,s,
\qquad
TZ2_{R2}=0.3\,s\] satisfies this requirement. Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{
\text{(C)}\;
TZ2_{R1}=0.3\,\text{s},\;
TZ2_{R2}=0.3\,\text{s}
}\]
